-CH2-CH2-CHO.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Conjugate cyanide addition supplies the fourth carbon while preserving an aldehyde for subsequent Strecker chemistry. This is a speculative prebiotic bottleneck: useful 1,4-selectivity under these conditions is not established here. Competing 1,2-cyanohydrin formation and acrolein polymerization could dominate. No selective yield is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of the terminal nitrile to succinic semialdehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions promoting nitrile hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-410 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Above water saturation pressure, typically 10-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic, anoxic fluid, approximately pH 1-3; sufficient residence for nitrile and amide hydrolysis, followed by cooling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile is hydrated through an amide and hydrolyzed to the carboxylic acid, releasing ammonia in formal neutral-species bookkeeping.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis has the required connectivity and atom balance. Selective preservation of the aldehyde throughout hot hydrolysis is not demonstrated here and is an important limitation. Succinic semialdehyde hydration, reversible cyclic forms and condensation can reduce its effective availability. Ammonia will largely be ammonium under the actual acidic conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker aminonitrile formation from succinic semialdehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide solution, approximately pH 8-9; cooled and rebuffered separately from the preceding acidic hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine or iminium species with the aldehyde; cyanide addition produces the alpha-aminonitrile that contains the complete glutamate carbon skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is standard Strecker connectivity applied to a carboxylated aldehyde. The new nitrile carbon becomes glutamate's alpha-carboxyl carbon after hydrolysis. Aldehyde hydration, competing cyanohydrin formation and intramolecular chemistry can limit conversion. The reaction does not select L-glutamate and is not presented as a validated integrated prebiotic sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glutamate Strecker aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions promoting nitrile hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Above water saturation pressure, typically 10-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3; strongly aqueous rather than drying conditions; cool after conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Successive nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis replace the nitrile with a carboxyl group while retaining the alpha-amino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the conventional amino-acid-producing endpoint of Strecker chemistry. Preparative hydrolysis often requires stronger acid or longer heating than a natural fluid can reliably provide. Competing lactam formation, including pyroglutamate formation after glutamic acid appears, makes residence time and product preservation important.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type capture of the carboxylated aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich, carbonate/bicarbonate-bearing pool, approximately pH 8-9; dissolved inorganic carbon is represented by its CO2-equivalent formula.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbamate formation from the aminonitrile and carbon dioxide is followed by cyclization and rearrangement to a substituted hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Bucherer-Bergs chemistry provides a genuine alternative to immediate aminonitrile hydrolysis by storing the amino-acid precursor in a hydantoin ring. Its application to this particular mixed prebiotic solution is a chemical extrapolation, not a demonstrated environmental yield. Hydantoin formation may improve precursor persistence while making final hydrolysis more difficult.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis and decarbamoylation of carboxyethylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide supplied by alkaline hydrothermal fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"395-445 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Above water saturation pressure, typically 20-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Base-promoted hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline fluid with nominal cooled-sample pH approximately 10-11; potentially prolonged residence, followed by cooling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydantoin ring opening gives an N-carbamoyl amino acid, followed by hydrolytic decarbamoylation to glutamic acid, ammonia and carbon dioxide equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis is an established amino-acid synthesis transformation, but useful rates at this comparatively modest geochemical alkalinity are unverified. Preparative reactions commonly employ substantially stronger acid or base. This is therefore a conditional kinetic bottleneck, not a demonstrated high-yield vent reaction. More severe treatment may destroy or cyclize the product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker reaction before side-chain nitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide enrichment, approximately pH 8-9; limited heating preserves the pre-existing nitrile.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation and cyanide addition at the aldehyde generate 2-aminopentanedinitrile while leaving the original side-chain nitrile intact.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This chemically distinct processing order avoids having to preserve succinic semialdehyde through an earlier nitrile-hydrolysis stage. Aldehyde Strecker chemistry supports the transformation, but environmental selectivity and intermediate accumulation remain unverified. Both nitriles must eventually be hydrolyzed, and no stereochemical preference is expected.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Complete hydrolysis of 2-aminopentanedinitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions promoting nitrile and amide hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-425 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Above water saturation pressure, typically 10-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3; hydrolysis of both nitriles is required; quench before extensive amino-acid degradation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Both nitrile groups undergo hydration and amide hydrolysis, producing the two carboxyl groups of glutamic acid and two ammonia equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The dinitrile has exactly the carbon and nitrogen connectivity required for glutamate. Complete rather than partial hydrolysis is essential; monoamide and monoacid products can accumulate. Whether acidic natural fluids provide adequate reaction time without substantial decomposition is an open kinetic question.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type capture of the aminodinitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich bicarbonate/carbonate solution, approximately pH 8-9; conditions mild enough to retain the remote nitrile.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The alpha-aminonitrile group captures carbon dioxide and cyclizes into a hydantoin, leaving the remote nitrile as a cyanoethyl substituent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This branch applies established hydantoin-forming reaction logic to the aminodinitrile rather than its carboxylated counterpart. It is distinct from direct dinitrile hydrolysis because the alpha-amino-acid precursor is temporarily incorporated into a ring. Useful prebiotic formation and persistence of this specific compound are not established here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Complete hydrolysis of cyanoethylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide supplied by alkaline hydrothermal fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"395-445 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Above water saturation pressure, typically 20-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Base-promoted hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Nominal cooled-sample pH approximately 10-11; prolonged hydrolysis may be necessary; rapid cooling after product formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"This net transformation combines side-chain nitrile hydrolysis, hydantoin ring opening and removal of the carbamoyl group, yielding glutamic acid, ammonia and carbon dioxide equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Complete hydrolysis has the correct atom balance and produces glutamate without adding further skeletal carbon. However, it combines two demanding hydrolytic requirements. Rates at the stated alkalinity, accumulation of partial hydrolysates and survival of glutamate are unverified. This branch is explicitly conditional rather than a demonstrated prebiotic process.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-mediated net C2+C3 assembly of 2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved Fe2+ providing coordination and, in this candidate redox closure, reducing equivalents\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+ coordination; Fe2+ is also consumed in the modeled net redox balance\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, iron-rich geothermal pool; approximately pH 5-7; precipitation of oxidized iron is allowed; no molecular oxygen is supplied.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A coarse-grained C-C addition and subsequent redox/dehydration sequence joins glyoxylate and pyruvate into the C5 keto-diacid skeleton. The modeled two-electron reduction is balanced by Fe2+ oxidation to a ferric oxyhydroxide sink.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Iron-promoted pyruvate/glyoxylate networks experimentally access 2-oxoglutarate among numerous products (Muchowska, Varma and Moran, 2019). This is not a claim that the displayed net equation is a demonstrated elementary mechanism. In particular, FeOOH is a candidate redox-bookkeeping sink, not an experimentally established unique coproduct for that study. The exact electron source, iron phases and pathway distribution under these specified conditions require verification; substrate consumption cannot be equated with selective 2-oxoglutarate yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron-driven reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native Fe(0) surface acting as a consumed reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich, anoxic pore water, approximately pH 6-9; accessible native or meteoritic iron; protons denote formal redox bookkeeping rather than an independently concentrated acid feed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the keto group and forms an imine/iminium intermediate. Reduction coupled to Fe(0) oxidation installs the alpha-amino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reductive amination of alpha-keto acids is a chemically supported route to amino acids, and zero-valent iron can supply the necessary electrons. Efficient selective conversion of 2-oxoglutarate on an unmodified natural iron surface under this exact scenario is not established here. Iron passivation, hydrogen evolution, competing carbonyl reduction and decarboxylation are limitations. Native iron availability is conditional despite the broader abundance of Fe2+.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven mineral-surface reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Nickel-bearing metallic surface; awaruite is a candidate natural catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar total pressure with appreciable hydrogen partial pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Nickel-bearing metal, provisionally awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, hydrogen-rich serpentinization fluid, approximately pH 8-10; ammonia enrichment and limited sulfide poisoning are required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia-derived imine formation is followed by surface activation of hydrogen and hydrogenation of the C=N functionality.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Catalytic hydrogenative reductive amination is established reaction chemistry, and nickel-bearing metals provide a chemically justified means of activating H2. Selective glutamate formation on natural awaruite under these conditions is a speculative geochemical extrapolation, not a demonstrated catalyst-substrate result asserted here. Competing ketone hydrogenation, mineral poisoning and low imine abundance may prevent useful conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-base equilibration to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_024\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cooled, buffered prebiotic water at pH 6-8; background buffer accepts the released proton.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proton redistribution and net loss of one proton from the neutral glutamic-acid formula give predominantly the species with two carboxylates and one ammonium group, carrying net charge -1.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is well-established solution speciation rather than a separate carbon-skeleton synthesis. It makes the target's protonation state explicit and is shared by all ten pathways. Neither this equilibrium nor the preceding achiral chemistry provides an intrinsic preference for L-glutamate.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Crossed-aldol acrolein source followed by semialdehyde Strecker synthesis\",\n      \"description\": \"Form acrolein from formaldehyde and acetaldehyde, extend it by conjugate cyanide addition, hydrolyze the first nitrile, then perform Strecker synthesis and final hydrolysis. Conditional chiefly on conjugate hydrocyanation selectivity and aldehyde survival.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Crossed-aldol acrolein source followed by carboxyethylhydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the semialdehyde-first carbon assembly sequence, but capture its Strecker aminonitrile as a carboxyethylhydantoin before amino-acid release. Adds the conditional hydantoin-hydrolysis bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Crossed-aldol acrolein source followed by aminodinitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Perform the aldehyde Strecker reaction before hydrolyzing the side-chain nitrile, then hydrolyze both nitriles together. Avoids the requirement to isolate succinic semialdehyde.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Crossed-aldol acrolein source followed by cyanoethylhydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate the aminodinitrile, capture its alpha-aminonitrile group in a hydantoin ring, then hydrolyze the ring and remote nitrile. Product release under plausible alkalinity remains unverified.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycerol-derived acrolein followed by semialdehyde Strecker synthesis\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate acrolein in a separate high-temperature glycerol-dehydration setting, quench it, and use the semialdehyde-first Strecker branch. Requires glycerol availability and effective hot-to-cool transport.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycerol-derived acrolein followed by carboxyethylhydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Combine thermal glycerol dehydration with the semialdehyde-first hydantoin branch. This is a distinct feedstock-conversion history sharing downstream chemistry with P2, not an independently validated complete synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycerol-derived acrolein followed by aminodinitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Quench glycerol-derived acrolein, construct the aminodinitrile without prior nitrile hydrolysis, and release glutamate by complete hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycerol-derived acrolein followed by cyanoethylhydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Combine glycerol-derived acrolein with aminodinitrile formation and temporary hydantoin storage before complete hydrolysis. Both the hydrocyanation selectivity and final hydrolysis remain critical uncertainties.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-mediated keto-diacid assembly and native-iron reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Join glyoxylate and pyruvate through a literature-inspired iron-mediated network, then use consumed Fe(0) to drive reductive amination. The modeled iron redox closure and mineral-specific selectivity require experimental verification.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_024\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-mediated keto-diacid assembly and hydrogen-driven reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the same C2+C3 keto-diacid assembly, followed by hydrogenative reductive amination on a candidate nickel-bearing mineral surface. This differs from P9 in its electron source and catalytic requirement.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_024\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_019\",\n    \"mol_020\",\n    \"mol_023\",\n    \"mol_024\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glutamate, racemic and predominantly monoanionic at pH 6-8", "overall_strategy": "Construct glutamate from explicitly supplied C1-C3 organic feedstocks, hydrogen cyanide, ammonia and inorganic reductants. Eight graph-distinct pathways combine two chemically different acrolein sources with four downstream Strecker/hydantoin processing branches. Two additional pathways use iron-mediated formation of 2-oxoglutarate followed by different reductive-amination chemistries. These are ten candidate reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses. Unverified selectivities, mineral performance and hydrolysis rates are identified in the reaction reasoning. Feedstock availability is a boundary assumption; this network does not establish their planetary abundance. Incompatible stages require transport, cooling and changes in pH rather than a single reaction vessel. Neutral acid formulas provide atom-balance bookkeeping; the final acid-base reaction represents equilibration to glutamate. No enantioselection is implied. Some reaction nodes describe net multistep transformations rather than elementary events. Ferric oxyhydroxide is a terminal redox coproduct assigned the schema's intermediate role because no coproduct role is available.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "Ethanal", "common_name": "Acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C3H8O3", "iupac_name": "Propane-1,2,3-triol", "common_name": "Glycerol", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H8O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h3-6H,1-2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Ammonia", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-Oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-Oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "Iron", "common_name": "Native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "Fe2+", "iupac_name": "Iron(II) ion", "common_name": "Ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "FeHO2", "iupac_name": "Iron(III) oxide hydroxide", "common_name": "Ferric oxyhydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "Hydron", "common_name": "Proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "3-Hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "3-Hydroxypropionaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C3H4O", "iupac_name": "Prop-2-enal", "common_name": "Acrolein", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O/c1-2-3-4/h2-3H,1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C4H5NO", "iupac_name": "4-Oxobutanenitrile", "common_name": "3-Cyanopropanal", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C4H6O3", "iupac_name": "4-Oxobutanoic acid", "common_name": "Succinic semialdehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C5H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "4-Amino-4-cyanobutanoic acid", "common_name": "Glutamate Strecker aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C5H7N3", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopentanedinitrile", "common_name": "2-Aminoglutaronitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C6H8N2O4", "iupac_name": "3-(2,4-Dioxoimidazolidin-5-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "5-(2-Carboxyethyl)hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C6H7N3O2", "iupac_name": "3-(2,4-Dioxoimidazolidin-5-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "5-(2-Cyanoethyl)hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C5H6O5", "iupac_name": "2-Oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-Oxoglutaric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "C5H9NO4", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Glutamic acid, unspecified stereochemistry", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "C5H8NO4-", "iupac_name": "2-Azaniumylpentanedioate", "common_name": "Glutamate, unspecified stereochemistry", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Crossed aldol addition of acetaldehyde to formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved hydroxide acting as a base catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly alkaline, anoxic pool; approximately pH 9-11; staged carbonyl supply and mineral screening from direct UV."}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde enolate attacks formaldehyde, forming the C-C bond of 3-hydroxypropanal after proton transfer.", "reasoning": "This is conventional crossed-aldol chemistry with the correct C2+C1 connectivity for acrolein production. It is a candidate prebiotic implementation, not an established selective environmental synthesis. Acetaldehyde self-condensation, formaldehyde disproportionation and product oligomerization compete strongly; selective accumulation is a bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Dehydration of 3-hydroxypropanal to acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions acting as an acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic volcanic pool or concentrating film, approximately pH 2-4; requires transfer from the alkaline aldol setting and prompt capture of volatile acrolein."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted elimination of water from the beta-hydroxy aldehyde produces the conjugated aldehyde acrolein.", "reasoning": "Formation of a conjugated carbonyl provides a chemical driving force for aldol dehydration. Useful conversion at these mild environmental conditions remains conditional: acrolein can volatilize, hydrate, polymerize or undergo photochemical destruction. This reaction is not assumed to proceed selectively in the original alkaline mixture."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Hot-compressed-water dehydration of glycerol to acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Acidic hydrothermal fluid providing Br\u00f8nsted-acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "540-600 K", "pressure": "150-300 bar", "catalyst": "Br\u00f8nsted acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Short residence in hot compressed water, followed by rapid quenching and transport to cooler water; amino acids must not accompany this high-temperature stage."}, "mechanism": "Sequential acid-catalyzed dehydration of glycerol removes two water equivalents and generates acrolein.", "reasoning": "Glycerol-to-acrolein dehydration is established chemical processing chemistry, including hot-water implementations. Its geochemical application is conditional on glycerol availability, suitable acidity and rapid product removal. Hydroxyacetone formation, fragmentation and polymerization compete. This is a chemically different acrolein source, not evidence that ordinary mild surface pools generate acrolein efficiently."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Candidate conjugate hydrocyanation of acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8-9, permitting some cyanide anion; sheltered pores or covered pools limit HCN and acrolein loss. Hydrothermal acrolein must first be quenched."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide undergoes 1,4-addition to the activated alkene; protonation of the resulting enolate gives NC-CH2-CH2-CHO.", "reasoning": "Conjugate cyanide addition supplies the fourth carbon while preserving an aldehyde for subsequent Strecker chemistry. This is a speculative prebiotic bottleneck: useful 1,4-selectivity under these conditions is not established here. Competing 1,2-cyanohydrin formation and acrolein polymerization could dominate. No selective yield is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydrolysis of the terminal nitrile to succinic semialdehyde", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions promoting nitrile hydrolysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-410 K", "pressure": "Above water saturation pressure, typically 10-100 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic, anoxic fluid, approximately pH 1-3; sufficient residence for nitrile and amide hydrolysis, followed by cooling."}, "mechanism": "The nitrile is hydrated through an amide and hydrolyzed to the carboxylic acid, releasing ammonia in formal neutral-species bookkeeping.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydrolysis has the required connectivity and atom balance. Selective preservation of the aldehyde throughout hot hydrolysis is not demonstrated here and is an important limitation. Succinic semialdehyde hydration, reversible cyclic forms and condensation can reduce its effective availability. Ammonia will largely be ammonium under the actual acidic conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Strecker aminonitrile formation from succinic semialdehyde", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide solution, approximately pH 8-9; cooled and rebuffered separately from the preceding acidic hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine or iminium species with the aldehyde; cyanide addition produces the alpha-aminonitrile that contains the complete glutamate carbon skeleton.", "reasoning": "This is standard Strecker connectivity applied to a carboxylated aldehyde. The new nitrile carbon becomes glutamate's alpha-carboxyl carbon after hydrolysis. Aldehyde hydration, competing cyanohydrin formation and intramolecular chemistry can limit conversion. The reaction does not select L-glutamate and is not presented as a validated integrated prebiotic sequence."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrolysis of glutamate Strecker aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_006"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions promoting nitrile hydrolysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-420 K", "pressure": "Above water saturation pressure, typically 10-100 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3; strongly aqueous rather than drying conditions; cool after conversion."}, "mechanism": "Successive nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis replace the nitrile with a carboxyl group while retaining the alpha-amino group.", "reasoning": "This is the conventional amino-acid-producing endpoint of Strecker chemistry. Preparative hydrolysis often requires stronger acid or longer heating than a natural fluid can reliably provide. Competing lactam formation, including pyroglutamate formation after glutamic acid appears, makes residence time and product preservation important."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs-type capture of the carboxylated aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich, carbonate/bicarbonate-bearing pool, approximately pH 8-9; dissolved inorganic carbon is represented by its CO2-equivalent formula."}, "mechanism": "Carbamate formation from the aminonitrile and carbon dioxide is followed by cyclization and rearrangement to a substituted hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Bucherer-Bergs chemistry provides a genuine alternative to immediate aminonitrile hydrolysis by storing the amino-acid precursor in a hydantoin ring. Its application to this particular mixed prebiotic solution is a chemical extrapolation, not a demonstrated environmental yield. Hydantoin formation may improve precursor persistence while making final hydrolysis more difficult."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydrolysis and decarbamoylation of carboxyethylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide supplied by alkaline hydrothermal fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "395-445 K", "pressure": "Above water saturation pressure, typically 20-100 bar", "catalyst": "Base-promoted hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline fluid with nominal cooled-sample pH approximately 10-11; potentially prolonged residence, followed by cooling."}, "mechanism": "Hydantoin ring opening gives an N-carbamoyl amino acid, followed by hydrolytic decarbamoylation to glutamic acid, ammonia and carbon dioxide equivalents.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis is an established amino-acid synthesis transformation, but useful rates at this comparatively modest geochemical alkalinity are unverified. Preparative reactions commonly employ substantially stronger acid or base. This is therefore a conditional kinetic bottleneck, not a demonstrated high-yield vent reaction. More severe treatment may destroy or cyclize the product."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Strecker reaction before side-chain nitrile hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_006", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide enrichment, approximately pH 8-9; limited heating preserves the pre-existing nitrile."}, "mechanism": "Imine formation and cyanide addition at the aldehyde generate 2-aminopentanedinitrile while leaving the original side-chain nitrile intact.", "reasoning": "This chemically distinct processing order avoids having to preserve succinic semialdehyde through an earlier nitrile-hydrolysis stage. Aldehyde Strecker chemistry supports the transformation, but environmental selectivity and intermediate accumulation remain unverified. Both nitriles must eventually be hydrolyzed, and no stereochemical preference is expected."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Complete hydrolysis of 2-aminopentanedinitrile", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_006"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions promoting nitrile and amide hydrolysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-425 K", "pressure": "Above water saturation pressure, typically 10-100 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3; hydrolysis of both nitriles is required; quench before extensive amino-acid degradation."}, "mechanism": "Both nitrile groups undergo hydration and amide hydrolysis, producing the two carboxyl groups of glutamic acid and two ammonia equivalents.", "reasoning": "The dinitrile has exactly the carbon and nitrogen connectivity required for glutamate. Complete rather than partial hydrolysis is essential; monoamide and monoacid products can accumulate. Whether acidic natural fluids provide adequate reaction time without substantial decomposition is an open kinetic question."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs-type capture of the aminodinitrile", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich bicarbonate/carbonate solution, approximately pH 8-9; conditions mild enough to retain the remote nitrile."}, "mechanism": "The alpha-aminonitrile group captures carbon dioxide and cyclizes into a hydantoin, leaving the remote nitrile as a cyanoethyl substituent.", "reasoning": "This branch applies established hydantoin-forming reaction logic to the aminodinitrile rather than its carboxylated counterpart. It is distinct from direct dinitrile hydrolysis because the alpha-amino-acid precursor is temporarily incorporated into a ring. Useful prebiotic formation and persistence of this specific compound are not established here."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Complete hydrolysis of cyanoethylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide supplied by alkaline hydrothermal fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "395-445 K", "pressure": "Above water saturation pressure, typically 20-100 bar", "catalyst": "Base-promoted hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Nominal cooled-sample pH approximately 10-11; prolonged hydrolysis may be necessary; rapid cooling after product formation."}, "mechanism": "This net transformation combines side-chain nitrile hydrolysis, hydantoin ring opening and removal of the carbamoyl group, yielding glutamic acid, ammonia and carbon dioxide equivalents.", "reasoning": "Complete hydrolysis has the correct atom balance and produces glutamate without adding further skeletal carbon. However, it combines two demanding hydrolytic requirements. Rates at the stated alkalinity, accumulation of partial hydrolysates and survival of glutamate are unverified. This branch is explicitly conditional rather than a demonstrated prebiotic process."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Iron-mediated net C2+C3 assembly of 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_013", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Fe2+ providing coordination and, in this candidate redox closure, reducing equivalents"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Fe2+ coordination; Fe2+ is also consumed in the modeled net redox balance", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, iron-rich geothermal pool; approximately pH 5-7; precipitation of oxidized iron is allowed; no molecular oxygen is supplied."}, "mechanism": "A coarse-grained C-C addition and subsequent redox/dehydration sequence joins glyoxylate and pyruvate into the C5 keto-diacid skeleton. The modeled two-electron reduction is balanced by Fe2+ oxidation to a ferric oxyhydroxide sink.", "reasoning": "Iron-promoted pyruvate/glyoxylate networks experimentally access 2-oxoglutarate among numerous products (Muchowska, Varma and Moran, 2019). This is not a claim that the displayed net equation is a demonstrated elementary mechanism. In particular, FeOOH is a candidate redox-bookkeeping sink, not an experimentally established unique coproduct for that study. The exact electron source, iron phases and pathway distribution under these specified conditions require verification; substrate consumption cannot be equated with selective 2-oxoglutarate yield."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Native-iron-driven reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_012", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native Fe(0) surface acting as a consumed reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K", "pressure": "10-100 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich, anoxic pore water, approximately pH 6-9; accessible native or meteoritic iron; protons denote formal redox bookkeeping rather than an independently concentrated acid feed."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the keto group and forms an imine/iminium intermediate. Reduction coupled to Fe(0) oxidation installs the alpha-amino group.", "reasoning": "Reductive amination of alpha-keto acids is a chemically supported route to amino acids, and zero-valent iron can supply the necessary electrons. Efficient selective conversion of 2-oxoglutarate on an unmodified natural iron surface under this exact scenario is not established here. Iron passivation, hydrogen evolution, competing carbonyl reduction and decarboxylation are limitations. Native iron availability is conditional despite the broader abundance of Fe2+."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Hydrogen-driven mineral-surface reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_006", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Nickel-bearing metallic surface; awaruite is a candidate natural catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-420 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar total pressure with appreciable hydrogen partial pressure", "catalyst": "Nickel-bearing metal, provisionally awaruite", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, hydrogen-rich serpentinization fluid, approximately pH 8-10; ammonia enrichment and limited sulfide poisoning are required."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia-derived imine formation is followed by surface activation of hydrogen and hydrogenation of the C=N functionality.", "reasoning": "Catalytic hydrogenative reductive amination is established reaction chemistry, and nickel-bearing metals provide a chemically justified means of activating H2. Selective glutamate formation on natural awaruite under these conditions is a speculative geochemical extrapolation, not a demonstrated catalyst-substrate result asserted here. Competing ketone hydrogenation, mineral poisoning and low imine abundance may prevent useful conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Acid-base equilibration to glutamate", "inputs": ["mol_024"], "outputs": ["mol_025", "mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "290-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cooled, buffered prebiotic water at pH 6-8; background buffer accepts the released proton."}, "mechanism": "Proton redistribution and net loss of one proton from the neutral glutamic-acid formula give predominantly the species with two carboxylates and one ammonium group, carrying net charge -1.", "reasoning": "This is well-established solution speciation rather than a separate carbon-skeleton synthesis. It makes the target's protonation state explicit and is shared by all ten pathways. Neither this equilibrium nor the preceding achiral chemistry provides an intrinsic preference for L-glutamate."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Crossed-aldol acrolein source followed by semialdehyde Strecker synthesis", "description": "Form acrolein from formaldehyde and acetaldehyde, extend it by conjugate cyanide addition, hydrolyze the first nitrile, then perform Strecker synthesis and final hydrolysis. Conditional chiefly on conjugate hydrocyanation selectivity and aldehyde survival.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Crossed-aldol acrolein source followed by carboxyethylhydantoin processing", "description": "Use the semialdehyde-first carbon assembly sequence, but capture its Strecker aminonitrile as a carboxyethylhydantoin before amino-acid release. Adds the conditional hydantoin-hydrolysis bottleneck.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_021"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Crossed-aldol acrolein source followed by aminodinitrile hydrolysis", "description": "Perform the aldehyde Strecker reaction before hydrolyzing the side-chain nitrile, then hydrolyze both nitriles together. Avoids the requirement to isolate succinic semialdehyde.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_020"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Crossed-aldol acrolein source followed by cyanoethylhydantoin processing", "description": "Generate the aminodinitrile, capture its alpha-aminonitrile group in a hydantoin ring, then hydrolyze the ring and remote nitrile. Product release under plausible alkalinity remains unverified.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_010", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_017", "mol_020", "mol_022"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Glycerol-derived acrolein followed by semialdehyde Strecker synthesis", "description": "Generate acrolein in a separate high-temperature glycerol-dehydration setting, quench it, and use the semialdehyde-first Strecker branch. Requires glycerol availability and effective hot-to-cool transport.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_018", "mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Glycerol-derived acrolein followed by carboxyethylhydantoin processing", "description": "Combine thermal glycerol dehydration with the semialdehyde-first hydantoin branch. This is a distinct feedstock-conversion history sharing downstream chemistry with P2, not an independently validated complete synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_018", "mol_021"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Glycerol-derived acrolein followed by aminodinitrile hydrolysis", "description": "Quench glycerol-derived acrolein, construct the aminodinitrile without prior nitrile hydrolysis, and release glutamate by complete hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_020"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Glycerol-derived acrolein followed by cyanoethylhydantoin processing", "description": "Combine glycerol-derived acrolein with aminodinitrile formation and temporary hydantoin storage before complete hydrolysis. Both the hydrocyanation selectivity and final hydrolysis remain critical uncertainties.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_010", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_020", "mol_022"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Iron-mediated keto-diacid assembly and native-iron reductive amination", "description": "Join glyoxylate and pyruvate through a literature-inspired iron-mediated network, then use consumed Fe(0) to drive reductive amination. The modeled iron redox closure and mineral-specific selectivity require experimental verification.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_023", "mol_024"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Iron-mediated keto-diacid assembly and hydrogen-driv