: [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol addition\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved Fe2+ as a Lewis-acid and enolization promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Dissolved Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6 screening window in shaded geothermal water. Start with millimolar-to-tens-of-millimolar carbonyl acids. Exclude oxygen and limit phosphate or carbonate precipitation of iron. Residence time must be determined from formation and loss rates.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate enolization followed by addition to the glyoxylate aldehyde gives the C5 beta-hydroxy keto diacid. Reference-state equation: C3H4O3 + C2H2O3 -> C5H6O6.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) demonstrated an Fe(II)-promoted network from pyruvate and glyoxylate containing metabolic intermediates, including 2-oxoglutarate. This supports the feedstock combination and reaction-network context, but not quantitative isolation of every edge drawn here. Self-condensation, competing additions and decomposition must be measured. No clay or sulfide is added without a specific catalytic rationale.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate aldol addition to oxalomalate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 6-8. Use a cool, recently replenished carbonyl-acid pool. Oxaloacetate must react before substantial background decarboxylation; an assumed long-lived oxaloacetate reservoir is not acceptable.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The enolizable carbon of oxaloacetate adds to glyoxylate, forming oxalomalate. Reference-state equation: C4H4O5 + C2H2O3 -> C6H6O8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxalomalate formation from oxaloacetate and glyoxylate is documented in classical solution chemistry associated with Ruffo et al. (1962). That work is evidence for the chemical transformation, not for an early-Earth inventory or integrated synthesis. This is a genuine alternative carbon entry because oxaloacetate, rather than pyruvate, provides the nucleophilic carbon skeleton. Its principal liability is oxaloacetate instability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxalomalate decarboxylation to hydroxyoxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 5-7. Follow disappearance of oxalomalate and appearance of the C5 product directly. Carbon dioxide escape can favor decarboxylation but must not be mistaken for evidence of product selectivity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Beta-keto-acid decarboxylation removes the pendant carboxyl group, followed by proton transfer and keto-enol equilibration. Reference-state equation: C6H6O8 -> C5H6O6 + CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The oxalomalate chemistry associated with Ruffo et al. (1962) provides the relevant nonenzymatic condensation/decarboxylation precedent. The equation identifies the lost carbon explicitly and does not require iron oxidation. Product recovery and competing fragmentation under the proposed geothermal conditions remain unquantified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxyoxoglutarate dehydration to an unsaturated keto diacid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved Fe2+ as a candidate Lewis-acid promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Dissolved Fe2+, proposed\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6. Use a concentrated aqueous film rather than assuming complete drying. Determine whether dehydration can outcompete retro-aldol cleavage and decarboxylation. E/Z composition is unspecified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Elimination of water from the beta-hydroxy ketoacid forms the conjugated keto-alkene. Reference-state equation: C5H6O6 -> C5H4O5 + H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically reasonable elementary decomposition of the overall carbon-assembly chemistry motivated by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019), but the selected isolated intermediate and operating window are not claimed to have been validated by that study. It is a conditional edge. Separating dehydration from reduction avoids presenting selective assembly and deoxygenation as one unexplained reaction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Explicit Fe(0)-driven reduction of the conjugated carbon-carbon double bond\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh native or meteoritic Fe(0) surfaces, consumed as reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6, anoxic and mildly acidic enough to limit passivation. Native iron availability is local, not inferred from abundant Fe2+. Quantify Fe dissolution, carbonyl reduction, double-bond reduction and product destruction separately.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Candidate surface electron transfer and protonation saturate the activated C=C bond while retaining the keto group. Balanced equation: C5H4O5 + Fe + 2 H+ -> C5H6O5 + Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the shared, unresolved selectivity bottleneck. Native Fe supplies an explicit two-electron budget; Fe2+ is the immediate dissolved oxidation product, so neither FeOOH formation nor an unspecified H2 catalyst is assumed. Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) support the broader formation of 2-oxoglutarate in an iron-promoted network, not this exact isolated-enone reduction by native iron. This deliberate mechanistic proposal requires substrate-specific testing before any complete pathway is called established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia condensation with 2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in low-gas-exchange water-filled pores\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 6.5-8 and concentrated total ammonia/ammonium. Free NH3, not total ammonium alone, controls nucleophilic availability. The imine is an equilibrium intermediate and is not assumed isolable.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia addition gives a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to an imine or its protonated forms. Reference-state equation: C5H6O5 + NH3 -> C5H7NO4 + H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is standard carbonyl-imine chemistry, but substantial steady-state accumulation of this particular imine in an anoxic geothermal mixture has not been established by the evidence cited here. Hydration, imine hydrolysis and ammonia loss can suppress the productive branch. It is retained as a conditional direct-ammonia route, not conflated with the better-supported hydroxylamine route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe(0)-driven reduction of the 2-oxoglutarate imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh native or meteoritic Fe(0), consumed as reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in a low-gas-exchange aqueous compartment\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 6-7 with the ammonia pool retained. Couple reduction to ongoing imine formation rather than transferring an isolated imine. Screen iron passivation and direct ketoacid-to-hydroxyacid reduction as competing processes.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Two-electron reduction and protonation of C=N yield racemic glutamic acid. Balanced equation: C5H7NO4 + Fe + 2 H+ -> C5H9NO4 + Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The electron accounting is explicit, but useful glutamate selectivity for this exact NH3/Fe(0) implementation is not established here. Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) used hydroxylamine and metallic iron for amino-acid formation; that result must not be presented as direct validation of ammonia-derived imine reduction. This branch needs its own kinetic and product-distribution measurements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"2-Oxoglutarate oxime formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6.5. Add hydroxylamine only after carbon-skeleton assembly because it also traps pyruvate and glyoxylate. Retain liquid water and avoid assuming photochemical persistence of hydroxylamine in an exposed pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with the keto group to form an oxime. Reference-state equation: C5H6O5 + H3NO -> C5H7NO5 + H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydroxylamine followed by metallic iron produced biological amino acids, including glutamate, in the reaction-network work of Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). Oxime formation is the chemically conventional interpretation of the first stage. Geological hydroxylamine supply and competition from other carbonyl compounds remain major limitations; no abundant hydroxylamine reservoir is asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxime reduction and N-O cleavage to glutamic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native or meteoritic Fe(0), consumed as reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6. Maintain reducing, oxygen-free conditions and accessible metal surfaces. Cool after conversion. Avoid extended hot, acidic or drying treatment that can promote glutamate cyclization or decomposition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxime reduction includes both C=N reduction and N-O bond cleavage. Balanced four-electron equation: C5H7NO5 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C5H9NO4 + H2O + 2 Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the most directly literature-grounded glutamate-forming endpoint in the network: Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) reported amino-acid production after hydroxylamine and metallic iron treatment. The split into oxime formation and reduction is mechanistic bookkeeping, not a claim of isolated-intermediate yield. Native-iron surface area, corrosion, recovery and compatibility with the upstream mixture still require measurement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate oxime formation for an alanine nitrogen shuttle\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6.5. Prepare the donor in a pyruvate-rich portion of the feed rather than assuming selective oximation of pyruvate in the complete carbonyl mixture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condensation at the pyruvate keto group forms its oxime. Reference-state equation: C3H4O3 + H3NO -> C3H5NO3 + H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The hydroxylamine/iron amino-acid synthesis of Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) supports alanine generation from this carbonyl pool. The nitrogen-shuttle branch has a chemical rationale: it separates reduction of a small ketoacid from subsequent amino-group transfer to 2-oxoglutarate. It is not evidence that the later transamination is productive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate oxime reduction to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native or meteoritic Fe(0), consumed as reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6. Remove residual metal grains by settling before evaluating transamination; this does not remove dissolved Fe2+. Donor production and recovery must be measured independently.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Four-electron oxime reduction produces racemic alanine. Balanced equation: C3H5NO3 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + H2O + 2 Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alanine formation after hydroxylamine/metallic-iron treatment is supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). The explicit four-electron balance avoids treating N-O cleavage as a two-electron imine reduction. No enantioselectivity is expected from the agents specified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate alanine-to-2-oxoglutarate transamination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved Fe2+ as a candidate Schiff-base coordination promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Dissolved Fe2+, proposed and unvalidated for this substrate pair\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 6-8. Test donor excess, reversibility, metal complexation and amino-acid degradation. Product pyruvate can re-enter carbon-skeleton assembly, but that coupling must not be assumed to overcome unfavorable kinetics.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed reversible Schiff-base formation, proton transfer and hydrolysis exchange the amino and keto groups. Balanced equation: C3H7NO2 + C5H6O5 -> C3H4O3 + C5H9NO4. No external redox equivalent is required.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative target-specific edge: the cited hydroxylamine/iron work does not establish useful nonenzymatic alanine-to-2-oxoglutarate transamination. Fe2+ is proposed because coordination can influence imine formation and proton transfer, but binding alone does not demonstrate catalysis. This branch tests an amino-group shuttle and must be rejected if measured rates, equilibrium or decomposition prevent glutamate accumulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate oxime formation for an aspartate nitrogen shuttle\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6.5. Use freshly supplied oxaloacetate and measure whether oximation outruns its decarboxylation. Do not assume the oxime is indefinitely stable.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine captures the oxaloacetate keto group. Reference-state equation: C4H4O5 + H3NO -> C4H5NO5 + H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aspartate production in the hydroxylamine/iron-treated network of Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) motivates this conventional precursor relationship. The evidence does not establish the lifetime or isolated yield of oxaloacetate oxime under the proposed conditions. Capturing a labile ketoacid is chemically motivated, but successful capture must be demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate oxime reduction to aspartate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native or meteoritic Fe(0), consumed as reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 4.5-6. Screen shorter residence times first because decarboxylation can redirect material toward C3 products. Quantify aspartate rather than inferring it from nitrogen incorporation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Four-electron reduction of the oxime gives racemic aspartic acid. Balanced equation: C4H5NO5 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C4H7NO4 + H2O + 2 Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The endpoint is motivated by aspartate formation reported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019), while the isolated substrate-specific rate and selectivity remain unverified here. Explicit Fe(0) consumption accounts for both imine reduction and N-O cleavage. Competing loss of a carboxyl group is an important branch-specific bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate aspartate-to-2-oxoglutarate transamination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved Fe2+ as a candidate Schiff-base coordination promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Dissolved Fe2+, proposed and unvalidated for this substrate pair\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 6-8. Lower temperatures limit oxaloacetate loss but may make transamination too slow. Measure both directions and distinguish catalytic turnover from stoichiometric metal-bound adduct formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed amino-group transfer through imine formation, proton rearrangement and hydrolysis. Balanced equation: C4H7NO4 + C5H6O5 -> C4H4O5 + C5H9NO4.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative target-specific edge: biological aspartate aminotransferase chemistry is not evidence that Fe2+ alone performs the same transformation at useful rates. The branch is chemically motivated by transfer between two ketoacid/amino-acid pairs already present in the network. Oxaloacetate consumption could affect equilibrium, but neither that effect nor catalyst performance is assumed sufficient.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide/ammonia/CO2-enabled conversion of 2-oxoglutarate to glutamic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved CO2/inorganic carbon as a promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar with retained dissolved inorganic carbon and limited gas exchange\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CO2/inorganic-carbon promoter; not assigned as a reducing mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use an ammonia-containing, mildly alkaline solution, provisionally pH 8-9, while verifying the published substrate-specific protocol before implementation. HCN/NH3 retention and CO2 speciation are essential. Dissolved iron can sequester cyanide; settling metal grains alone does not solve that incompatibility. This branch is conditional on demonstrated iron management and carbon/nitrogen mass balance.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Represented as an overall cyanide/ammonia/inorganic-carbon-enabled transformation, not an asserted elementary hydantoin mechanism. A formal productive balance retaining the operational ammonia and CO2 pools is: C5H6O5 + HCN + NH3 + H2O + CO2 -> C5H9NO4 + NH3 + 2 CO2. After cancellation, the formal net is C5H6O5 + HCN + H2O -> C5H9NO4 + CO2. This closure does not assign atom provenance and requires coproduct and isotope verification.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen and Krishnamurthy (2022) reported amino-acid formation from alpha-ketoacids using cyanide, ammonia and CO2. This provides a literature-supported alternative reaction family without inventing an unvalidated hydantoin-opening sequence. The formal coproduct closure written here is not presented as a measured atom-mapping result. Target-specific protocol verification, cyanide sequestration by residual iron, volatile retention and recovery from the upstream mixture remain unresolved; therefore the integrated pathways using this edge are conditional.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Final acid-base equilibration to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Equilibrate in pH 6-8 water after cooling. Avoid prolonged hot drying or strongly acidic treatment. Buffer capacity must accommodate proton release; no biological machinery or chiral selection is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reference-state deprotonation: C5H9NO4 -> C5H8NO4- + H+. At pH 6-8, both carboxyl groups are predominantly deprotonated and the amino group predominantly protonated, giving net charge -1.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is ordinary acid-base speciation, supported by glutamic-acid pKa values of approximately 2.2, 4.3 and 9.7. It does not require an enzyme, oxygen or a mineral catalyst. The product is racemic because no preceding step specifies a source of enantioselection. Cooling and avoiding harsh dehydration reduce, but do not quantitatively eliminate, cyclization to pyroglutamate.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with oxime reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Construct the C5 skeleton from pyruvate and glyoxylate, then install nitrogen through hydroxylamine capture and Fe(0)-driven oxime reduction. The glutamate-forming endpoint has direct literature support; the isolated dehydration/reduction implementation and full integration remain conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with oxime reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Form oxalomalate from oxaloacetate and glyoxylate, lose one carbon as CO2, and use the shared oxime-reduction endpoint. This is a distinct carbon-entry sequence, not a different name for P1. Oxaloacetate availability and survival add an upstream limitation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with direct ammonia reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the pyruvate-derived C5 backbone, reversible ammonia-derived imine formation and explicit two-electron reduction by Fe(0). Conditional on both selective backbone reduction and productive imine reduction; hydroxylamine results do not validate this ammonia branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with direct ammonia reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Reach the ammonia/imine endpoint through oxalomalate formation and decarboxylation. This candidate combines oxaloacetate instability with the unvalidated target-specific NH3/Fe(0) reductive-amination implementation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with an alanine nitrogen shuttle\",\n      \"description\": \"Prepare 2-oxoglutarate through the pyruvate entry and prepare alanine from a separate portion of pyruvate feed. Candidate transamination transfers nitrogen to the C5 ketoacid and regenerates pyruvate. The sequence includes both branches needed at the transamination junction; the transfer step is speculative.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with an alanine nitrogen shuttle\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate the C5 acceptor through oxalomalate while generating the alanine donor from supplied pyruvate. This is a distinct carbon-entry route converging on the same proposed amino-group shuttle. Nonenzymatic target-specific transamination remains unestablished.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with an aspartate nitrogen shuttle\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate 2-oxoglutarate from pyruvate and glyoxylate, and generate aspartate from supplied oxaloacetate through its oxime. Candidate amino-group transfer produces glutamic acid and regenerates oxaloacetate. Donor preparation and the speculative transfer reaction require separate validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with an aspartate nitrogen shuttle\",\n      \"description\": \"Use separate portions of oxaloacetate feed for oxalomalate-based carbon assembly and for aspartate preparation. Transfer the amino group to 2-oxoglutarate. Shared feedstock does not imply selective one-pot operation; both oxaloacetate loss and the proposed transamination are important limitations.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with cyanide/ammonia/CO2-enabled amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate the ketoacid from pyruvate and glyoxylate, then apply the literature-motivated cyanide/ammonia/inorganic-carbon reaction family. The pathway is conditional on substrate-specific protocol confirmation, verified coproduct balance and successful management of iron-cyanide incompatibility.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with cyanide/ammonia/CO2-enabled amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Reach the cyanide-enabled endpoint through oxalomalate formation and decarboxylation. CO2 formed upstream may contribute to an inorganic-carbon pool, but retention, concentration and correct speciation are not assumed. This remains an integrated candidate, not a demonstrated ten-step-independent origin.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_011\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_022\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glutamate, racemic, predominantly the monoanion at pH 6-8", "overall_strategy": "This is a connected candidate synthesis network, not a claim that ten complete prebiotic syntheses have been experimentally established. Ten distinct reaction sequences arise from two chemically different carbon-skeleton entry routes and five nitrogen-installation routes. Pyruvate plus glyoxylate, or oxaloacetate plus glyoxylate followed by decarboxylation, converge on 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate. Dehydration and explicitly electron-balanced reduction lead to 2-oxoglutarate. Nitrogen is introduced through an oxime, an imine, alanine-mediated transamination, aspartate-mediated transamination, or cyanide/ammonia/CO2-enabled chemistry. All stages are assigned to anoxic surface geothermal waters; adding a hydrothermal stage is not chemically necessary. Small carbonyl-acid feedstocks, ammonia, hydroxylamine, cyanide, and locally available native or meteoritic iron are boundary assumptions, not demonstrated co-located inventories. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not measured optima or yield predictions. Neutral acid formulas provide reference-state bookkeeping; actual solution speciation follows the stated pH, and H+ represents buffered proton exchange. Native Fe is consumed as an electron donor, with dissolved Fe2+ explicitly produced; no unspecified hydrogen-activating mineral or FeOOH endpoint is assumed. Selective enone reduction, some nitrogen-installation alternatives, residence times, separation losses, and integration remain unresolved. Consequently, this network does not justify a complete-network confidence of 0.9. Stoichiometric equations are recorded in mechanism strings without repeating molecule IDs in input or output arrays.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C4H4O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "Oxaloacetic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4O5/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "Hydroxylamine", "common_name": "Hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "Hydron", "common_name": "Buffered proton equivalent", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "Iron", "common_name": "Native or meteoritic iron, Fe(0)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "Iron(2+)", "common_name": "Dissolved ferrous iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C6H6O8", "iupac_name": "1-hydroxy-3-oxopropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid", "common_name": "Oxalomalic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H6O6", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-4-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "4-Hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H4O5", "iupac_name": "4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid", "common_name": "2-Oxopent-3-enedioic acid, using biochemical numbering", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H6O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-Oxoglutaric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H7NO4", "iupac_name": "2-iminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-Oxoglutarate imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C5H7NO5", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-Oxoglutarate oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C3H5NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)propanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvate oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-Alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C4H5NO5", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)butanedioic acid", "common_name": "Oxaloacetate oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C4H7NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "DL-Aspartic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H7NO4/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h2H,1,5H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C5H9NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "DL-Glutamic acid, reference protonation state", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C5H8NO4-", "iupac_name": "2-azaniumylpentanedioate", "common_name": "DL-Glutamate", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol addition", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Fe2+ as a Lewis-acid and enolization promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Dissolved Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6 screening window in shaded geothermal water. Start with millimolar-to-tens-of-millimolar carbonyl acids. Exclude oxygen and limit phosphate or carbonate precipitation of iron. Residence time must be determined from formation and loss rates."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate enolization followed by addition to the glyoxylate aldehyde gives the C5 beta-hydroxy keto diacid. Reference-state equation: C3H4O3 + C2H2O3 -> C5H6O6.", "reasoning": "Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) demonstrated an Fe(II)-promoted network from pyruvate and glyoxylate containing metabolic intermediates, including 2-oxoglutarate. This supports the feedstock combination and reaction-network context, but not quantitative isolation of every edge drawn here. Self-condensation, competing additions and decomposition must be measured. No clay or sulfide is added without a specific catalytic rationale."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate aldol addition to oxalomalate", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 6-8. Use a cool, recently replenished carbonyl-acid pool. Oxaloacetate must react before substantial background decarboxylation; an assumed long-lived oxaloacetate reservoir is not acceptable."}, "mechanism": "The enolizable carbon of oxaloacetate adds to glyoxylate, forming oxalomalate. Reference-state equation: C4H4O5 + C2H2O3 -> C6H6O8.", "reasoning": "Oxalomalate formation from oxaloacetate and glyoxylate is documented in classical solution chemistry associated with Ruffo et al. (1962). That work is evidence for the chemical transformation, not for an early-Earth inventory or integrated synthesis. This is a genuine alternative carbon entry because oxaloacetate, rather than pyruvate, provides the nucleophilic carbon skeleton. Its principal liability is oxaloacetate instability."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Oxalomalate decarboxylation to hydroxyoxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 5-7. Follow disappearance of oxalomalate and appearance of the C5 product directly. Carbon dioxide escape can favor decarboxylation but must not be mistaken for evidence of product selectivity."}, "mechanism": "Beta-keto-acid decarboxylation removes the pendant carboxyl group, followed by proton transfer and keto-enol equilibration. Reference-state equation: C6H6O8 -> C5H6O6 + CO2.", "reasoning": "The oxalomalate chemistry associated with Ruffo et al. (1962) provides the relevant nonenzymatic condensation/decarboxylation precedent. The equation identifies the lost carbon explicitly and does not require iron oxidation. Product recovery and competing fragmentation under the proposed geothermal conditions remain unquantified."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Hydroxyoxoglutarate dehydration to an unsaturated keto diacid", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Fe2+ as a candidate Lewis-acid promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Dissolved Fe2+, proposed", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6. Use a concentrated aqueous film rather than assuming complete drying. Determine whether dehydration can outcompete retro-aldol cleavage and decarboxylation. E/Z composition is unspecified."}, "mechanism": "Elimination of water from the beta-hydroxy ketoacid forms the conjugated keto-alkene. Reference-state equation: C5H6O6 -> C5H4O5 + H2O.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically reasonable elementary decomposition of the overall carbon-assembly chemistry motivated by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019), but the selected isolated intermediate and operating window are not claimed to have been validated by that study. It is a conditional edge. Separating dehydration from reduction avoids presenting selective assembly and deoxygenation as one unexplained reaction."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Explicit Fe(0)-driven reduction of the conjugated carbon-carbon double bond", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_009", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fresh native or meteoritic Fe(0) surfaces, consumed as reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6, anoxic and mildly acidic enough to limit passivation. Native iron availability is local, not inferred from abundant Fe2+. Quantify Fe dissolution, carbonyl reduction, double-bond reduction and product destruction separately."}, "mechanism": "Candidate surface electron transfer and protonation saturate the activated C=C bond while retaining the keto group. Balanced equation: C5H4O5 + Fe + 2 H+ -> C5H6O5 + Fe2+.", "reasoning": "This is the shared, unresolved selectivity bottleneck. Native Fe supplies an explicit two-electron budget; Fe2+ is the immediate dissolved oxidation product, so neither FeOOH formation nor an unspecified H2 catalyst is assumed. Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) support the broader formation of 2-oxoglutarate in an iron-promoted network, not this exact isolated-enone reduction by native iron. This deliberate mechanistic proposal requires substrate-specific testing before any complete pathway is called established."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Ammonia condensation with 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in low-gas-exchange water-filled pores", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 6.5-8 and concentrated total ammonia/ammonium. Free NH3, not total ammonium alone, controls nucleophilic availability. The imine is an equilibrium intermediate and is not assumed isolable."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia addition gives a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to an imine or its protonated forms. Reference-state equation: C5H6O5 + NH3 -> C5H7NO4 + H2O.", "reasoning": "This is standard carbonyl-imine chemistry, but substantial steady-state accumulation of this particular imine in an anoxic geothermal mixture has not been established by the evidence cited here. Hydration, imine hydrolysis and ammonia loss can suppress the productive branch. It is retained as a conditional direct-ammonia route, not conflated with the better-supported hydroxylamine route."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Fe(0)-driven reduction of the 2-oxoglutarate imine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_009", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fresh native or meteoritic Fe(0), consumed as reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in a low-gas-exchange aqueous compartment", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 6-7 with the ammonia pool retained. Couple reduction to ongoing imine formation rather than transferring an isolated imine. Screen iron passivation and direct ketoacid-to-hydroxyacid reduction as competing processes."}, "mechanism": "Two-electron reduction and protonation of C=N yield racemic glutamic acid. Balanced equation: C5H7NO4 + Fe + 2 H+ -> C5H9NO4 + Fe2+.", "reasoning": "The electron accounting is explicit, but useful glutamate selectivity for this exact NH3/Fe(0) implementation is not established here. Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) used hydroxylamine and metallic iron for amino-acid formation; that result must not be presented as direct validation of ammonia-derived imine reduction. This branch needs its own kinetic and product-distribution measurements."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "2-Oxoglutarate oxime formation", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6.5. Add hydroxylamine only after carbon-skeleton assembly because it also traps pyruvate and glyoxylate. Retain liquid water and avoid assuming photochemical persistence of hydroxylamine in an exposed pool."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine condenses with the keto group to form an oxime. Reference-state equation: C5H6O5 + H3NO -> C5H7NO5 + H2O.", "reasoning": "Hydroxylamine followed by metallic iron produced biological amino acids, including glutamate, in the reaction-network work of Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). Oxime formation is the chemically conventional interpretation of the first stage. Geological hydroxylamine supply and competition from other carbonyl compounds remain major limitations; no abundant hydroxylamine reservoir is asserted."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Oxime reduction and N-O cleavage to glutamic acid", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_009", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Native or meteoritic Fe(0), consumed as reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6. Maintain reducing, oxygen-free conditions and accessible metal surfaces. Cool after conversion. Avoid extended hot, acidic or drying treatment that can promote glutamate cyclization or decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Oxime reduction includes both C=N reduction and N-O bond cleavage. Balanced four-electron equation: C5H7NO5 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C5H9NO4 + H2O + 2 Fe2+.", "reasoning": "This is the most directly literature-grounded glutamate-forming endpoint in the network: Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) reported amino-acid production after hydroxylamine and metallic iron treatment. The split into oxime formation and reduction is mechanistic bookkeeping, not a claim of isolated-intermediate yield. Native-iron surface area, corrosion, recovery and compatibility with the upstream mixture still require measurement."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Pyruvate oxime formation for an alanine nitrogen shuttle", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6.5. Prepare the donor in a pyruvate-rich portion of the feed rather than assuming selective oximation of pyruvate in the complete carbonyl mixture."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine condensation at the pyruvate keto group forms its oxime. Reference-state equation: C3H4O3 + H3NO -> C3H5NO3 + H2O.", "reasoning": "The hydroxylamine/iron amino-acid synthesis of Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) supports alanine generation from this carbonyl pool. The nitrogen-shuttle branch has a chemical rationale: it separates reduction of a small ketoacid from subsequent amino-group transfer to 2-oxoglutarate. It is not evidence that the later transamination is productive."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Pyruvate oxime reduction to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_009", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_004", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Native or meteoritic Fe(0), consumed as reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6. Remove residual metal grains by settling before evaluating transamination; this does not remove dissolved Fe2+. Donor production and recovery must be measured independently."}, "mechanism": "Four-electron oxime reduction produces racemic alanine. Balanced equation: C3H5NO3 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + H2O + 2 Fe2+.", "reasoning": "Alanine formation after hydroxylamine/metallic-iron treatment is supported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019). The explicit four-electron balance avoids treating N-O cleavage as a two-electron imine reduction. No enantioselectivity is expected from the agents specified."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Candidate alanine-to-2-oxoglutarate transamination", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_002", "mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Fe2+ as a candidate Schiff-base coordination promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Dissolved Fe2+, proposed and unvalidated for this substrate pair", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 6-8. Test donor excess, reversibility, metal complexation and amino-acid degradation. Product pyruvate can re-enter carbon-skeleton assembly, but that coupling must not be assumed to overcome unfavorable kinetics."}, "mechanism": "Proposed reversible Schiff-base formation, proton transfer and hydrolysis exchange the amino and keto groups. Balanced equation: C3H7NO2 + C5H6O5 -> C3H4O3 + C5H9NO4. No external redox equivalent is required.", "reasoning": "Speculative target-specific edge: the cited hydroxylamine/iron work does not establish useful nonenzymatic alanine-to-2-oxoglutarate transamination. Fe2+ is proposed because coordination can influence imine formation and proton transfer, but binding alone does not demonstrate catalysis. This branch tests an amino-group shuttle and must be rejected if measured rates, equilibrium or decomposition prevent glutamate accumulation."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Oxaloacetate oxime formation for an aspartate nitrogen shuttle", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6.5. Use freshly supplied oxaloacetate and measure whether oximation outruns its decarboxylation. Do not assume the oxime is indefinitely stable."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine captures the oxaloacetate keto group. Reference-state equation: C4H4O5 + H3NO -> C4H5NO5 + H2O.", "reasoning": "Aspartate production in the hydroxylamine/iron-treated network of Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019) motivates this conventional precursor relationship. The evidence does not establish the lifetime or isolated yield of oxaloacetate oxime under the proposed conditions. Capturing a labile ketoacid is chemically motivated, but successful capture must be demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Oxaloacetate oxime reduction to aspartate", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_009", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_004", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Native or meteoritic Fe(0), consumed as reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 4.5-6. Screen shorter residence times first because decarboxylation can redirect material toward C3 products. Quantify aspartate rather than inferring it from nitrogen incorporation."}, "mechanism": "Four-electron reduction of the oxime gives racemic aspartic acid. Balanced equation: C4H5NO5 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C4H7NO4 + H2O + 2 Fe2+.", "reasoning": "The endpoint is motivated by aspartate formation reported by Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019), while the isolated substrate-specific rate and selectivity remain unverified here. Explicit Fe(0) consumption accounts for both imine reduction and N-O cleavage. Competing loss of a carboxyl group is an important branch-specific bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Candidate aspartate-to-2-oxoglutarate transamination", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_003", "mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Fe2+ as a candidate Schiff-base coordination promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Dissolved Fe2+, proposed and unvalidated for this substrate pair", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 6-8. Lower temperatures limit oxaloacetate loss but may make transamination too slow. Measure both directions and distinguish catalytic turnover from stoichiometric metal-bound adduct formation."}, "mechanism": "Proposed amino-group transfer through imine formation, proton rearrangement and hydrolysis. Balanced equation: C4H7NO4 + C5H6O5 -> C4H4O5 + C5H9NO4.", "reasoning": "Speculative target-specific edge: biological aspartate aminotransferase chemistry is not evidence that Fe2+ alone performs the same transformation at useful rates. The branch is chemically motivated by transfer between two ketoacid/amino-acid pairs already present in the network. Oxaloacetate consumption could affect equilibrium, but neither that effect nor catalyst performance is assumed sufficient."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Cyanide/ammonia/CO2-enabled conversion of 2-oxoglutarate to glutamic acid", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_007", "mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_005", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved CO2/inorganic carbon as a promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar with retained dissolved inorganic carbon and limited gas exchange", "catalyst": "CO2/inorganic-carbon promoter; not assigned as a reducing mineral", "aqueous": true, "other": "Use an ammonia-containing, mildly alkaline solution, provisionally pH 8-9, while verifying the published substrate-specific protocol before implementation. HCN/NH3 retention and CO2 speciation are essential. Dissolved iron can sequester cyanide; settling metal grains alone does not solve that incompatibility. This branch is conditional on demonstrated iron management and carbon/nitrogen mass balance."}, "mechanism": "Represented as an overall cyanide/ammonia/inorganic-carbon-enabled transformation, not an asserted elementary hydantoin mechanism. A formal productive balance retaining the operational ammonia and CO2 pools is: C5H6O5 + HCN + NH3 + H2O + CO2 -> C5H9NO4 + NH3 + 2 CO2. After cancellation, the formal net is C5H6O5 + HCN + H2O -> C5H9NO4 + CO2. This closure does not assign atom provenance and requires coproduct and isotope verification.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen and Krishnamurthy (2022) reported amino-acid formation from alpha-ketoacids using cyanide, ammonia and CO2. This provides a literature-supported alternative reaction family without inventing an unvalidated hydantoin-opening sequence. The formal coproduct closure written here is not presented as a measured atom-mapping result. Target-specific protocol verification, cyanide sequestration by residual iron, volatile retention and recovery from the upstream mixture remain unresolved; therefore the integrated pathways using this edge are conditional."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Final acid-base equilibration to glutamate", "inputs": ["mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Equilibrate in pH 6-8 water after cooling. Avoid prolonged hot drying or strongly acidic treatment. Buffer capacity must accommodate proton release; no biological machinery or chiral selection is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Reference-state deprotonation: C5H9NO4 -> C5H8NO4- + H+. At pH 6-8, both carboxyl groups are predominantly deprotonated and the amino group predominantly protonated, giving net charge -1.", "reasoning": "This is ordinary acid-base speciation, supported by glutamic-acid pKa values of approximately 2.2, 4.3 and 9.7. It does not require an enzyme, oxygen or a mineral catalyst. The product is racemic because no preceding step specifies a source of enantioselection. Cooling and avoiding harsh dehydration reduce, but do not quantitatively eliminate, cyclization to pyroglutamate."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with oxime reduction", "description": "Construct the C5 skeleton from pyruvate and glyoxylate, then install nitrogen through hydroxylamine capture and Fe(0)-driven oxime reduction. The glutamate-forming endpoint has direct literature support; the isolated dehydration/reduction implementation and full integration remain conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with oxime reduction", "description": "Form oxalomalate from oxaloacetate and glyoxylate, lose one carbon as CO2, and use the shared oxime-reduction endpoint. This is a distinct carbon-entry sequence, not a different name for P1. Oxaloacetate availability and survival add an upstream limitation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with direct ammonia reductive amination", "description": "Use the pyruvate-derived C5 backbone, reversible ammonia-derived imine formation and explicit two-electron reduction by Fe(0). Conditional on both selective backbone reduction and productive imine reduction; hydroxylamine results do not validate this ammonia branch.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_015", "mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with direct ammonia reductive amination", "description": "Reach the ammonia/imine endpoint through oxalomalate formation and decarboxylation. This candidate combines oxaloacetate instability with the unvalidated target-specific NH3/Fe(0) reductive-amination implementation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_015", "mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with an alanine nitrogen shuttle", "description": "Prepare 2-oxoglutarate through the pyruvate entry and prepare alanine from a separate portion of pyruvate feed. Candidate transamination transfers nitrogen to the C5 ketoacid and regenerates pyruvate. The sequence includes both branches needed at the transamination junction; the transfer step is speculative.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015", "mol_018", "mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with an alanine nitrogen shuttle", "description": "Generate the C5 acceptor through oxalomalate while generating the alanine donor from supplied pyruvate. This is a distinct carbon-entry route converging on the same proposed amino-group shuttle. Nonenzymatic target-specific transamination remains unestablished.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_015", "mol_018", "mol_019"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Pyruvate-glyoxylate entry with an aspartate nitrogen shuttle", "description": "Generate 2-oxoglutarate from pyruvate and glyoxylate, and generate aspartate from supplied oxaloacetate through its oxime. Candidate amino-group transfer produces glutamic acid and regenerates oxaloacetate. Donor preparation and the speculative transfer reaction require separate validation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_013", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015", "mol_020", "mol_021"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Oxaloacetate-glyoxylate entry with an aspartate nitrogen shuttle", "description": "Use separate portions of oxaloacetate feed for oxalomalate-based carbon assembly and for aspartate preparation. Transfer the amino group to 2-oxoglutarate. Shared feedstock does not imply selective one-pot operation; both oxaloacetate loss and the proposed transamination are important limitations.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "r