m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glutamate, racemic, predominantly the monoanion at pH 6-8\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This is a connected candidate synthesis network, not a claim that ten complete prebiotic syntheses have been experimentally established. Ten distinct reaction sequences arise from two chemically different carbon-skeleton entry routes and five nitrogen-installation routes. Pyruvate plus glyoxylate, or oxaloacetate plus glyoxylate followed by decarboxylation, converge on 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate. Dehydration and explicitly electron-balanced reduction lead to 2-oxoglutarate. Nitrogen is introduced through an oxime, an imine, alanine-mediated transamination, aspartate-mediated transamination, or cyanide/ammonia/CO2-enabled chemistry. All stages are assigned to anoxic surface geothermal waters; adding a hydrothermal stage is not chemically necessary. Small carbonyl-acid feedstocks, ammonia, hydroxylamine, cyanide, and locally available native or meteoritic iron are boundary assumptions, not demonstrated co-located inventories. Numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not measured optima or yield predictions. Neutral acid formulas provide reference-state bookkeeping; actual solution speciation follows the stated pH, and H+ represents buffered proton exchange. Native Fe is consumed as an electron donor, with dissolved Fe2+ explicitly produced; no unspecified hydrogen-activating mineral or FeOOH endpoint is assumed. Selective enone reduction, some nitrogen-installation alternatives, residence times, separation losses, and integration remain unresolved. Consequently, this network does not justify a complete-network confidence of 0.9. Stoichiometric equations are recorded in mechanism strings without repeating molecule IDs in input or output arrays.\n\nMOLECULES (23 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C2H2O3 (Glyoxylic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_002: C3H4O3 (Pyruvic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_003: C4H4O5 (Oxaloacetic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_005: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_006: H3NO (Hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_007: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_008: H+ (Buffered proton equivalent) [starting]\n  - mol_009: Fe (Native or meteoritic iron, Fe(0)) [starting]\n  - mol_010: Fe+2 (Dissolved ferrous iron) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_011: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C6H6O8 (Oxalomalic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C5H6O6 (4-Hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C5H4O5 (2-Oxopent-3-enedioic acid, using biochemical numbering) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C5H6O5 (2-Oxoglutaric acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C5H7NO4 (2-Oxoglutarate imine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C5H7NO5 (2-Oxoglutarate oxime) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C3H5NO3 (Pyruvate oxime) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: C3H7NO2 (DL-Alanine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C4H5NO5 (Oxaloacetate oxime) [intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C4H7NO4 (DL-Aspartic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_022: C5H9NO4 (DL-Glutamic acid, reference protonation state) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_023: C5H8NO4- (DL-Glutamate) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (17 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_002, mol_001 -> mol_013 (Pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol addition) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_003, mol_001 -> mol_012 (Oxaloacetat